## THE COMMISSION
### Item 1 · B479-1: the web's own environment (commit 1: `web/scripts/e2e.mjs` and `web/src/e2e-runner.test.ts` only)
Make the runner **enforce, not assume**, that the web server Playwright starts presents the API's default caller, in BOTH live modes (`--live-api <url>` and `--live-api-order-check`).
**Requirements (statements, not code; the design is yours to choose and record in DESIGN):**
1. **Decide and record: REFUSE or REMOVE (Stamp slot B479-1 SHAPE; default REFUSE).** REFUSE = if any of the web's dev sign-in names is set in the runner's own environment, the run exits 2 naming the variable NAMES (never a value) and the precondition (the module `web/e2e/live-api-preconditions.mjs` states the premise), before any network call, any marker read or write, any server start. REMOVE = the child's environment lacks them and one line says which names were removed. Say in DESIGN why the default (the file's header says it "ENFORCES [the preconditions] and names the precondition it refused on"; a silent removal changes the operator's environment unseen; a refusal costs the operator one `unset`). **List the exact names from source** (re-grep `bff.ts` and `playwright.config.ts` at main; expected the four above) and justify each as in or out of the refusal set: the DEV_AUTH pair is the finding; MODE and BASE_URL are set by Playwright's command and are in the set only if you show (by a row, not by assertion) that a stale value cannot reach a live pass; either way the child env never carries the DEV_AUTH pair.
2. **One implementation, exported, tested (the B502 lesson).** The name list, the problem lines and the child-env builder are exported pure functions called by the run paths; there is no second copy of the logic in `liveApiRun`, `liveApiOrderCheck` or `playUnit`. The refusal for item 1.1 is evaluated **first** in `liveApiRun` (before `markerStep(process.env)`, which may create a per-user marker directory) and first in the order check (before `liveApiDataDir`, which creates a directory). `playUnit` builds its `env` from the exported builder (which drops the names whatever the caller did), so a future path that skips the refusal still cannot hand them on. Existing exports keep their names and signatures (an added OPTIONAL parameter is allowed; no existing row is edited).
3. **Behaviour rows, not text rows.** `web/src/e2e-runner.test.ts` gains rows (new rows only, in the existing live-API `describe`; no existing row is edited, deleted or loosened, except the import-list line that gains the new names: print it) proving:
   - the pure function: each name set alone -> a problem line naming it and no value; both set -> both named; none set -> `[]`; an empty-string value counts as SET for REFUSE (state why) or is dropped for REMOVE; the line never contains a value (use a canary value and assert absence);
   - the child-env builder drops the DEV_AUTH pair even when the input holds them and keeps `E2E_LIVE_API`, `E2E_TIMED`, `PLAYWRIGHT_HTML_OUTPUT_DIR` and (when asked) `PLAYWRIGHT_JSON_OUTPUT_NAME`;
   - **a CLI-level row** (this closes the gap B507-1 named for B502's rows: a source-text pin lets run-path mutants stay green): spawn `process.execPath` with the argument array `['scripts/e2e.mjs', '--live-api', 'http://127.0.0.1:8560', '--viewport', 'desktop']` (fixed text, `cwd` the web directory, no shell, an explicit child `env` object built in the test, a timeout) with the DEV_AUTH pair set to canaries and `E2E_LIVE_API_MARKER` pointing into a directory the test creates with `mkdtempSync`: it must exit 2, stderr must name the variables and must not contain the canary values, and the scratch directory must stay EMPTY (nothing written before the refusal); the CONTROL row is the same call with the pair unset: it must **not** print the env refusal (it reaches the API read and fails on the closed loopback port with the existing message, rc 2) - so the first row is red for the right reason; the same two rows for `--live-api-order-check --api-port 8561`. No row starts a server or touches anything but loopback port numbers in your block; every row passes under BOTH vitest pools (`--pool=forks` and `--pool=threads`: the rows set no `process.env` in the test process, the child env is explicit).
